WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:07.574
GPUI is the GPU-rendered Rust UI framework behind the Zed editor. It expects you to write your interface in Rust.

00:07.924 --> 00:13.244
With Osprey's UI machines, a GPUI app's whole interface is one data file instead.

00:14.444 --> 00:17.299
This casting player runs in a GPUI window.

00:17.649 --> 00:24.665
Every pixel comes from player.machine.json: its inputs, its state machines, its view and its skins.

00:25.865 --> 00:35.332
egui redraws Rust code every frame. Slint has its own markup language. Tauri puts a web front end in a web view. GPUI uses Rust views.

00:35.682 --> 00:44.765
A UI machine writes every state down, and names every transition, so the interface can be checked, tested without a window, and reskinned without code.

00:45.965 --> 00:50.470
The player has four layers: receivers, playback, commands and menu.

00:50.820 --> 00:57.882
Entering connecting requests cast.start with the file and the receiver. When the TV reports playing, the layer moves to playing.

00:58.232 --> 01:02.189
The document never says how the work is done. It holds no code at all.

01:03.389 --> 01:06.876
A skin can carry a canvas, drawn from an image atlas.

01:07.226 --> 01:15.386
This hit area is stamped once per TV found, and exists only while the receivers menu is open. Switching skins keeps every input and state.

01:16.586 --> 01:22.320
The Osprey code that remains answers each request, and feeds what the TV reports back in as inputs.

01:22.670 --> 01:24.914
It never decides what the screen shows.

01:26.114 --> 01:33.693
The whole document is validated when it loads. A transition to a misspelled state is refused, with its exact JSON path.

01:34.043 --> 01:39.550
A cycle is reported instead of spinning, and a refused document shows its problems in place of the app.

01:40.750 --> 01:49.572
The player runs in the GPUI host on macOS today. Osprey is actively evolving. Try it on your projects, and read the UI machines spec.
